    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a clear focal point at the top of the order as **Jackson Merrill** (75.27) and **Gunnar Henderson** (71.25) anchor our offensive projections. **Jackson Merrill** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Michael McGreevy, where a projected SP_xERA of 4.51 and a strong platoon advantage (+13%) boost his ceiling, while **Gunnar Henderson** remains a top play despite the challenge of facing a lefty; the 16% park factor and the 8% order-of-batting bump outweigh the platoon resistance. Similarly, **Coby Mayo** (65.57) warrants a strong start against Jorge Lopez; while the SP_xERA of 4.42 is moderate, his 16% park adjustment and platoon leverage make him a high-upside play for power, even against a defensively savvy opponent.\n\nWe are paying close attention to **Josh Lowe** (53.09), whose score is buoyed by a 14% platoon advantage against Dylan Cease. Crucially, the presence of a \"slow delivery\" warning for Cease (88% stolen base potential) makes **Josh Lowe** a priority, aligning with our organizational mandate to weight elite speed and base-stealing opportunities heavily. Conversely, the most difficult decision of the day involved **Miguel Vargas** (51.54), who was the first player left on the bench. He performed well enough to contend, but the optimizer ultimately favored the higher-floor efficiency of our projected starters like **Marcell Ozuna** and **Heliot Ramos**, noting that **Miguel Vargas** faces a slightly tougher environment regarding catcher-hold percentage compared to our active OF options.\n\nRisk management remains a theme today, as several positions\u2014specifically **Drake Baldwin**, **Kyle Tucker**, **Heliot Ramos**, and **Marcell Ozuna**\u2014rely on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. While we are confident in these placements, their effective scores are sensitive to order shifts; should **Kyle Tucker** drop lower in the order against Chris Sale, his projection will tighten significantly given the difficulty of the matchup. Furthermore, while we are starting **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (56.35), managers should monitor the weather closely; his score is solid, but it leans heavily on the 7% dynamic platoon advantage, and any late-breaking wind shifts could deflate his HR potential in what remains a strategic play against a pitcher as sharp as Tyler Detmers.",
